1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
When you hand-clawed a pile of tools, you hit a problem fast. The more tools you expose, the

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:11,000
harder it gets for the model to pick the right one. This lesson covers how to design a toolset

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:17,000
that routes reliably and where teams usually get it wrong. You already know the three parts of a

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:23,000
tool definition from the descriptions lesson. Name to invoke, description to root, schema to

5
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:29,000
shape the arguments. So instead of re-walking the triad, let's put it to work on a real definition.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,000
Picture a tool named Billing Lookup, described only as Gets Billing Info, with a schema holding

7
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:43,000
one open string called Query. Every part is present, and every part fails. The name states

8
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:49,000
no action, the description gives Claude nothing to root on against neighboring tools, and the open

9
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:57,000
string invites invented arguments. That's the shift this lesson makes. Set design means tuning

10
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:05,000
name, description, and schema across every tool at once. So each definition has to hold up next to

11
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:12,000
its neighbors, not just on its own. Here's the core principle. A toolset is a menu the model

12
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:19,000
reads on every turn, so keep it short and sharp. Fewer, more capable tools reduce selection ambiguity

13
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:26,000
because the model has fewer lookalike options to weigh. Each tool should map to one clear action

14
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:32,000
with predictable inputs. When you spread the same capability across a sprawling surface of tools,

15
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:39,000
wrong tool calls get more likely, and the model wastes turns backing out of bad picks. So add a

16
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:45,000
tool only when the task genuinely needs it. Capability comes from sharp scoping, not from

17
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:51,000
sheer count. A smaller set where each tool earns its place almost always outperforms a big one.

18
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:59,000
A common mistake is building one tool per API endpoint. It feels flexible, but it just mirrors

19
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:06,000
your API surface, so the agent has to juggle many low-level calls to finish one task. That floods

20
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:12,000
the menu with near duplicates the model has to choose between. The better move is to design tools

21
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:19,000
for the workflow the agent actually runs. One tool can list, filter, and act under the hood,

22
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:25,000
matching a realistic task in a single call. Think about what the agent is trying to accomplish,

23
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:30,000
then build a tool shaped like that goal. You're not exposing your backend, you're giving the

24
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,000
model a clean set of actions that line up with real work. Let's make consolidation concrete.

25
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:42,000
When several operations touch the same resource, fold them into one parameterized tool instead of

26
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:49,000
shipping three near duplicates. Say you have create, review, and merge for a pull request.

27
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:55,000
Rather than three separate tools with overlapping descriptions, expose one tool with an action

28
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:02,000
parameter that picks the operation. Consolidation shrinks the menu and that directly cuts selection

29
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:09,000
ambiguity. The model reasons about one clear choice and a parameter, not several competing

30
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:15,000
tools that all look related. This is the same idea as before, just applied at the action level.

31
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:22,000
Fewer, more capable tools are easier to route among, and they leave less room for the model

32
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:29,000
to guess wrong. These three habits keep tools aligned to real agent work. First, match real

33
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:35,000
workflows. Build a schedule event tool that lists people and books the slot internally. Instead of

34
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:43,000
three raw endpoint calls, the agent has to chain together itself. Second, compile context. A single

35
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:48,000
tool can gather everything the model needs at once, rather than forcing a string of small reads.

36
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:56,000
Third, avoid thin wrappers. A tool that just wraps one API endpoint is rarely the right shape for an

37
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:02,000
agent, even when it's easy to generate. The through line is simple. Build tools for what the agent does,

38
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:08,000
not for what your API happens to expose. The description is still where you steer routing,

39
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:14,000
and you know its anatomy from the descriptions lesson. So here's the set level angle. When two

40
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:20,000
tools could plausibly fire, the when not to use line is what keeps the model off the wrong one,

41
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:24,000
and that line only earns its keep when it's written against the actual neighbors in your set.

42
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:30,000
Same with parameters. Explain how each one changes behavior, because that's often the detail that

43
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,000
separates two look-alike tools. And when wrong tool calls show up in an agentic loop, you already

44
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:43,000
know the debugging order. Rewrite the description before you touch anything else. A schema says

45
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:49,000
what's structurally valid. The description says when a call fits, and in a set, which call fits.

46
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:57,000
What a tool returns matters as much as what it accepts. Three habits keep responses useful. First,

47
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:03,000
return high signal output. Give back only the field's clawed needs, because bloated responses

48
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:09,000
waste context and bury the part that matters. Second, return stable identifiers. Hand back

49
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:15,000
durable slugs or unique identifiers the model can reference on a later call, and favor human

50
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:22,000
readable ones over cryptic codes. Third, offer verbosity controls. Let the caller ask for a

51
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:28,000
concise result or a detailed one, so the response carries only as much as the task needs. Good

52
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:33,000
inputs get the tool called correctly. Good outputs keep the rest of the loop efficient and focused.

53
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:41,000
Here's the part teams skip. You can't tell a good tool set from a bad one just by reading it. You

54
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:46,000
have to measure it. Build a batch of realistic multi-tool tasks and run the agent against them.

55
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:55,000
Track more than accuracy. Watch the tool call count, the tokens consumed, the runtime, and the

56
00:05:55,000 --> 00:06:00,000
errors. Then read the reasoning transcripts, because that's where ambiguous descriptions

57
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:07,000
show themselves. You'll see the model hesitate between two tools, or reach for the wrong one,

58
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:13,000
and the fix is usually a wording change. Adjust the description, rerun the evals, and compare.

59
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:18,000
That loop turns tool design from guesswork into something you can improve on purpose.

60
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:26,000
A coherent tool set is small, sharp, and built for real work. Fewer well-scoped tools route better

61
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:32,000
than a sprawling surface. Consolidate related actions. Design for the agent's workflow,

62
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:38,000
and let descriptions carry the when-to-use signal. Just remember, evaluations are how

63
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:44,000
you know the set is working. That covers designing a coherent tool set. I'll see you in the next one.

